 urban ones that  helped trump  second there were a lot of undecided  voters who chose trump at the last  minute  the only way to capture that is to keep  polling until the bitter end but even  then  there are still voters who only make up  their minds  in the voting booth but some of them  didn't even get that far  research suggests that in 2016 a lot of  people were discouraged from voting  because according to the polls clinton  looked like a shoeing  that might have caused some people to  stay home because they believed the race  was uncompetitive  holsters say they've learned from their  mistakes they better hope so because the  combination of clinton's loss and  trump's win  convinced a lot of people that polls are  just junk  but two years later the polls for the  u.s midterms helped repair some of the  damage  while 2016 was so many ways to miss in  the 2018 midterms  in the united states polls function very  well they  forecast a democratic landslide and that  was exactly  what we got  [Applause]  and now the polls are back with a  vengeance  a new polling on the 2020 presidential  race former vice president joe biden  with a  lead over president trump president  trump behind in two states the poll also  shows biden with a 10-point lead it  would  take a polling failure much much larger  than on the scale we saw on 2016 for  all of those numbers to be wrong but  david also says  a lot can still change and he's not  alone  there's a poll in early september going  to tell you accurately  what's going to happen in wisconsin in  november you know someone that's been in  the field for 20 years i don't think so  that doesn't mean we can't ever trust  polls there are ways you can verify them  and don't just read one poll read many  it's like getting more than one opinion  the accepted wisdom is that polls do a  good job of taking the pulse of public  opinion  are people worried about losing their  jobs do they care about racial  inequality  what about the pandemic and their health  frankly even with their limitations  which can be substantial  polls are still the best tool that  society has to get  information about what's going on with  the entire public  so let's bring back alan lickman and  find out whether he thinks trump will  win again  he's created what he calls the 13 keys  the keys to the white house  if trump loses out on six or more  lichmann says the president is gone  so number one is midterm elections the  republicans in 2018  did badly so that's strike one against  trump  number two is party nomination he didn't  have to fight for it  so score one for trump three is  incumbency  obviously that's a win for trump any  third parties running  nope he gets another key the short-term  economy  well the u.s is in a recession back for  the president  outlook for the long-term economy also  not good  any major policy changes from the  previous administration  there have been plenty another one for  trump key number eight is social unrest  and race relations in the u.s are awful  so trump loses out there too  then there's scandal my favorite key  oh my gosh only the third president in  history  ever to be impeached by the full house  plus all kinds of other scandals  that counts against him trump takes key  number ten  no foreign policy or military failures  but he loses out on number 11  which is foreign policy or military  successes  his initiatives in north korea and the  middle east and  syria have all gone for north yes he  killed general sulaimani but i guarantee  you  95 of the american people couldn't tell  you who he is  today it's not like killing osama bin  laden  the next one asks whether the incumbent  donald trump is inspirational and  charismatic  according to lickman he is a flashy  showman but he only appeals to a narrow  slice of the electorate  and finally is the challenger joe biden  charismatic and inspirational  lichmann isn't convinced joe biden is a  decent  empathetic guy but he's no barack obama  with john f kennedy so will trump remain  in the white house  according to lickman the president has  lost out on seven of the thirteen keys  one more than necessary to say sorry  trump this time  you're not gonna win so he's pretty  confident  but there's a catch lickman says two  things could throw out his  prediction russian hackers and what he  calls voter suppression by the trump  administration  and 2020 is far from an ordinary  election year  the pandemic and the ruined global  economy made sure of that  this race is still a tough one to call  no matter what the polls say  the original interview with alan  lichtman was conducted by our colleagues  at the take which is an al jazeera  podcast  you can listen to their episode about  polls wherever you get your podcasts  i'll see you next week
 

